<h2>Introduction: The Global South Is Writing Its Own Story with Keyboards and QR Codes</h2>

<p>The share of adults worldwide with a financial account rose from 51% in 2011 to 79% in 2024 (World Bank, *The Global Findex Database 2025*). That is real progress. But seen from another angle: to this day, roughly 1.3 billion adults still do not have so much as a bank account — and the great majority of them live in the Global South.</p>

<p>When traditional bank branches struggle to reach remote areas, and when a bank card or a credit record is not easy for an ordinary person to obtain, how do people enter the modern economy?</p>

<p>The answer the Global South has given does not lie inside the banking system. It lies in mobile networks, QR codes, and open-source models. The South did not copy the North's path of "build branches first, digitize later." Instead, it adopted mobile payments, digital identity, and affordable AI directly — bringing financial and intelligent services to a far wider population.</p>

<p>This article tries to trace that path itself: how the Global South has built up its own "digital livelihood," and what this process means for people, for governance, and for the idea of "the South."</p>

<p>Before going further, let us first be clear about the phrase "Global South."</p>

<hr />

<h2>Chapter 1  Definition First: What Is the "Global South"</h2>

<p>"Global South" is not the region south of the equator on a map. It is a political-economic concept, referring to the group of countries with a lower level of development, a history of colonization, and an aspiration to change the international order.</p>

<p>The term can be traced to 1969. The American left-wing activist Carl Oglesby first used "Global South" in the context of the anti-Vietnam War movement, pointing to "an intolerable social order produced by the domination of the North over the South." An earlier source is the French scholar Alfred Sauvy's "Third World," coined in 1952. In 1974, Mao Zedong proposed the "Three Worlds" division — another lineage. After the Cold War, "Third World" gradually faded, carrying overtones of hierarchy, and "Global South" took its place as a more neutral expression (China-US Focus; Wikipedia, "North–South divide").</p>

<p>There are roughly three ways of defining the "Global South":</p>

<p>The first is the geographic path. Asia, Africa, Latin America, and Oceania south of the 1980 "Brandt Line" (the rough North-South divide proposed in the Brandt Report) are placed in the South.</p>

<p>The second is the developmental path. It refers to developing countries and lower-middle-income countries — those whose economic indicators rank toward the back.</p>

<p>The third is the political-historical path. It refers to an identity group that shares a colonial past, demands change in the international order, and pursues autonomy over its own development. This path comes closest to the political meaning of "South": it is not a label for the poor, but a collective identity and a political claim.</p>

<p>The territory covered is broad: Asia (excluding a few developed economies such as Japan and South Korea), Africa, Latin America and the Caribbean, and the island states of Oceania. In the UN statistical framework, "Group of 77 + China," with 78 countries, is often used as the basic list of the Global South; the Group of 77 itself has now expanded to about 134 countries (DW Chinese; China-US Focus).</p>

<p>There is one controversy that cannot be avoided: does China count as part of the Global South?</p>

<p>China's official position identifies itself as a member of the "Global South" (see the global-governance white paper and its formulations released by Chinese authorities in June 2026). At the same time, China is the world's second-largest economy and lies in the Northern Hemisphere, and it has disagreements with some Southern countries on borders, the South China Sea, and other issues. Western academic judgment is more divided: some scholars hold that "Global South" is more an identity than an economic reality, while others explicitly question whether China can be counted as the South.</p>

<p>This very controversy is a footnote to "Global South" as a political concept: it is not a static map, but a group of people with "similar starting points and similar solutions," who are now participating in defining their own position. Only by understanding this can we understand the "financial exclusion" that follows.</p>

<hr />

<h2>Chapter 2  Why the South Began with "Financial Exclusion"</h2>

<p>Since the core feature of the "Global South" is a development gap and a colonial/post-colonial legacy, its most common livelihood reality is this: a considerable share of the population has not been taken up by the banking system.</p>

<p>Physical branches are scarce. In many villages of Sub-Saharan Africa and South Asia, the nearest bank branch may be a hundred kilometers away. Identity documents are also hard to obtain. According to the World Bank's *Global Findex Database 2025*, roughly 1.3 billion adults worldwide still have no bank account (data as of 2024), and a good share of them lack even official identity documents — let alone credit records. Among adults in developing countries, about one in four still has no bank account.</p>

<p>This can be understood as a kind of "leapfrog." The North's financial system is "banks first, then digitization" — branches, credit cards, and credit bureaus first, then mobile banking layered on top. In the South, by contrast, many people use mobile payments directly, on their first-ever contact with financial services. This is not "upgrading from 2.0 to 3.0," but "going straight from 0 to 1."</p>

<p>What does it mean to have no bank? It is hard to save, hard to remit money safely, hard to get formal credit, and hard to have government subsidies reach you precisely. An illness, a drought, an urgent school fee — any of these can push a family toward loan sharks or back into poverty.</p>

<p>This is the first real problem "digital livelihood" must face: bring the people whom the financial system has "ignored" into the modern economic network first.</p>

<p>How did the South do it? Look at three legs.</p>

<hr />

<h2>Chapter 3  Three Legs: How the South Found a Way Out of Financial Exclusion</h2>

<p>The South did not copy the North's path. Instead, it adopted Digital Public Infrastructure (DPI — public digital platforms for payments, identity, data sharing, and other services available to the whole nation) to build three legs of financial inclusion: payment, identity, and credit.</p>

<p>**Payment.** India's UPI (Unified Payments Interface, the real-time transfer system led by the Reserve Bank of India) is the representative case. According to the National Payments Corporation of India (NPCI), UPI processed 23.66 billion transactions in a single month in July 2026, worth about 29.88 trillion rupees. For a street vendor, one QR code pasted on the stall is enough: the customer scans and pays, and the money goes straight in — no POS terminal, no card-swipe fee, no need for change.</p>

<p>Brazil's Pix (the instant payment system launched by the Central Bank of Brazil) is equally impressive. In the full year 2025, Pix processed close to 80 billion transactions, worth more than 35 trillion reais (about US$6.87 trillion), with about 93% of adults using it. In the first quarter of 2025, person-to-business (P2B) transactions already accounted for about 41% of Pix's total volume, and merchant acceptance became the fastest-growing driver — small merchants gained a collection capability previously available only to large enterprises (Central Bank of Brazil; PCMI).</p>

<p>Africa's M-Pesa (the mobile wallet service launched by Kenya's Safaricom) shows something else: a smartphone is not required. A feature phone and a SIM card are enough to send money, pay bills, and save. By March 2026, M-Pesa had 40 million monthly active users in Kenya (official Safaricom data).</p>

<p>**Identity.** Without identity, it is hard to be "seen." India's Aadhaar (the biometric identity system) uses fingerprints and iris scans to issue a unique 12-digit identity number to every resident. As of 2026, Aadhaar had issued about 1.4 billion numbers, covering the vast majority of India's adults; since 2009, cumulative authentication transactions have exceeded 1.5 trillion (official UIDAI data; Press Information Bureau of India, 2025). Digital identity makes it easier to "be seen by the state," and makes welfare distribution, account opening, and subsidy collection reach each individual more precisely.</p>

<p>**Credit.** Without collateral, how does one get credit? Part of the South's thinking is to use data in place of collateral. A user's transaction flows and bill-payment records on a payment platform can become the basis for credit assessment, giving micro-merchants and low-income workers — who have no property, no guarantor, and no traditional credit record — their first real chance at formal credit.</p>

<p>The three legs share common traits: low entry barriers, wide coverage, and relatively low cost. They give relatively marginalized people a chance to enter the modern economic network.</p>

<hr />

<h2>Chapter 4  The Livelihood Substance: What These Digital Infrastructures Actually Changed</h2>

<p>Data is abstract; it only becomes real when it lands on people. What have these three legs changed in ordinary people's lives?</p>

<p>**Saving money.** In the past, a rural Kenyan woman receiving remittances from her husband in the city might have had to endure long distances, long waits, and high fees — and might even be skimmed by middlemen. Now a single M-Pesa account does the job; cross-border remittance costs have fallen markedly, and money arrives faster and more safely (GSMA; World Bank).</p>

<p>**Being included.** An Indian chai-stall owner who once dealt only in cash had difficulty proving his economic activity and thus borrowing. After he started accepting UPI payments, his transaction flows became a credit record, and he could begin to borrow small business loans. A small Brazilian merchant, who once could only watch the collection methods reserved for big companies, now solves it with a single Pix QR code.</p>

<p>Of course, "being seen" and "being included" are conveniences, but they are also a new kind of identity-binding — a boundary worth noting on its own.</p>

<p>**Being able to protect oneself.** Digital identity makes it easier for low-income residents to collect subsidies, access public services, and obtain official documents. In extreme situations — a disaster, an epidemic — the government can deliver resources more precisely to those who need them most (World Bank, *Global Findex Database 2025*, on digital identity and payment reach).</p>

<p>**Having learned to use them — and still learning.**</p>

<p>There is another change, often overlooked: in the very act of using these tools, people themselves are gaining skill.</p>

<p>A rural woman who had never used a smartphone learns to send money and pay bills with M-Pesa, and to note down every incoming payment. What she acquires is a life skill that was completely unfamiliar to her before.</p>

<p>An Indian street vendor begins to read his own transaction flows, understands what "credit" means, and learns how to use these records to seek a loan. This is less "being included" than learning, through participation, a new way to survive.</p>

<p>The Central Bank of Brazil noticed the same thing when promoting Pix: the low entry barrier of instant payment let a great many people — who once relied only on cash, or even feared digital tools — feel for the first time that "I can learn this too; I can keep up." This "I can do it" experience lasts longer than a single saving (Central Bank of Brazil; PCMI on Pix inclusion).</p>

<p>In other words, what digital livelihood offers is not just a "pipeline" for access, but also a "practice field." Technology does not decide on people's behalf; it gives ordinary people a little more ability to decide for themselves — which is a different thing from "putting money into an account."</p>

<p>But we should also look at this honestly: it is not a technological utopia.</p>

<p>First, the digital divide. There are always people with no phone, no ID, no digital know-how — or who simply live in a village with no signal. According to the World Bank's *Global Findex Database 2025*, the account-ownership rate in Sub-Saharan Africa is 58%, still below the global average of 79%.</p>

<p>Then, the gender gap. Women's account-ownership rates in many developing countries remain lower than men's, and mobile payments do not automatically bring empowerment. That the number of "accounts" has grown does not mean "who controls the account" has changed. Mobile payments put the account into the phone, but whose hand holds the phone, and who gets to spend the money, is a separate question.</p>

<p>And there are costs. When "credit" becomes a string of data and "identity" becomes a fingerprint, one question cannot be avoided: is technology serving people, or demanding that people accommodate it? An elderly person without a smartphone may, for that reason, fail to collect a subsidy that is rightfully his; a person unwilling (or unable) to let a face or iris into a database may meet extra barriers in opening an account or collecting benefits. The World Bank, in the *Global Findex Database 2025*, also reminds us that the roll-out of digital identity needs careful balancing between inclusion and privacy, between convenience and risk. This means that when digital livelihood's "entry barrier lowers," it is sometimes accompanied by "no other road being available" — a convenience for the majority, but possibly a new form of exclusion for the minority who cannot keep up. Technology saves people trouble, and at the same time quietly makes choices for them. This is the line most deserving of being honestly written down.</p>

<p>The conclusion is sobering: digital infrastructure has indeed improved the daily lives of many people — farmers, vendors, housewives, micro-merchants — but the "digital livelihood" answer sheet has so far only been started.</p>

<hr />

<h2>Chapter 5  Three Discourses: How the South Tells Its Own Digital Story</h2>

<p>While the technology advances, a discussion is also unfolding about "who gets to define the South." The South is moving from being an observed and aided "object" to being the "subject" that tells its own story. There are at least three parallel narratives at present:</p>

<p>**The anti-colonial, self-reliance discourse.** "We must build our own digital infrastructure and not depend on the outside." The success of India's DPI and Africa's mobile money is evidence of this autonomy — solving the South's problems in the South's own way.</p>

<p>**The development and poverty-reduction discourse.** "Digital inclusion is a legitimate livelihood project." This discourse binds digital technology to poverty alleviation, education, health care, and employment, emphasizing service to ordinary people's daily lives.</p>

<p>**The competition and pricing discourse.** The "low-cost open-source" narrative represented by DeepSeek was once a striking card in this hand — giving the South its first real chance to reach near-frontier AI at relatively low cost.</p>

<p>But from August 2026, a change arrived. DeepSeek adopted peak/off-peak pricing and raised prices for the V4 (the new-generation large model) series: V4-Pro output at ¥27 per million tokens during peak hours (a token is the smallest billing unit for AI, roughly one word or half a Chinese character) and ¥13.5 in off-peak hours; V4-Flash output at ¥9 in peak hours and ¥4.5 off-peak. Input has its own tiers; peak hours are Beijing time 9:00–12:00 and 14:00–18:00, and the off-peak price is about half the peak price (per DeepSeek's official August 2026 price-adjustment notice).</p>

<p>So a question arises: when the South's own AI also begins to adjust prices, how does the "low-cost equal access" narrative change?</p>

<p>On one hand, the price increase weakens the "equal access" force. On the other hand, even after the adjustment, DeepSeek V4-Pro's peak output price is about US$3.78 per million tokens (roughly estimated at an exchange rate of 7.15, 2026-08), compared with roughly US$25–$30 per million tokens for OpenAI GPT-5.x and Claude Opus 5 output — still a price advantage, at about one-sixth to one-seventh of theirs. "Relatively cheap" still holds; it is just no longer "almost free."</p>

<p>On yet another hand, the price adjustment itself signals a change: when an AI company in a Southern country gains pricing power because demand is strong, it has moved from being a "follower" to being "a supplier with bargaining power." This may be a concrete side-image of "the South standing on its own."</p>

<p>Still, price is only part of the threshold. For ordinary people in the South, "affordable" also means: can I ask questions in my own language, does it run on a not-so-smooth network, and are the answers actually usable? None of this can be written out on a price sheet. A lower price is one precondition for "equal access," but it is far from the whole of it.</p>

<p>These three discourses do not replace one another; they are parallel self-claims. Together they point to a more fundamental question: can the South participate in defining itself?</p>

<hr />

<h2>Conclusion: A Manuscript Still Being Written</h2>

<p>Back to the 1.3 billion people without a bank account at the outset.</p>

<p>The Global North has a mature banking system and large models, while the Global South is using "digital public infrastructure + mobile payments + affordable AI" to bring financial and intelligent services to a wider population. Neither path is superior to the other; they simply start from different places and find different solutions.</p>

<p>What is truly worth watching in the South is not its leaning toward one narrative or another, but that it has begun, in its own way, to answer "what is development, what is a good life" — with the plainest yardstick: has ordinary people's life actually gotten better?</p>

<p>A rural housewife opens her own account for the first time; a street vendor is accepted into the formal payment network for the first time; a small farmer asks an AI a question in his mother tongue for the first time. These everyday, trivial, still-unfolding facts are the clues to reading this path.</p>

<p>These things share one trait: tools have not replaced people; in the act of helping people get things done, they have also given people a little more ability to do things themselves. The most solid way to "write for oneself" may lie in this plainest form of "learning while using."</p>

<p>"Global South" has never been a static classification. It is a manuscript still being written. The digital divide remains, the risks remain, and the right to define is still under discussion. But the direction is clear: the South is spending its time, with keyboards, QR codes, and ever more open tools, writing down its own "digital livelihood."</p>

<p>This manuscript is worth continuing to read.</p>

